Row-Bot Voice (the voice extra) uses Pipecat (pipecat-ai 1.16, https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat,
Copyright (c) 2024-2026 Daily, BSD-2-Clause) for its local turn detection, with the model weights
that ship inside its wheel: Silero VAD (https://github.com/snakers4/silero-vad, MIT) and Smart Turn
v3.2 (https://github.com/pipecat-ai/smart-turn, BSD-2-Clause), run on onnxruntime. Pipecat brings
pipecat-resampler (BSD-2-Clause), loudness and sentencex (MIT), loguru (MIT), aiofiles
(Apache-2.0), Markdown (BSD-3-Clause), and num2words (https://github.com/savoirfairelinux/num2words,
GNU Lesser General Public License 2.1) with docopt (MIT). num2words is pure Python, installed as
its own package and used unmodified.

Row-Bot Voice's streaming speech-to-text runs on sherpa-onnx (sherpa-onnx and sherpa-onnx-core
2.0, https://github.com/k2-fsa/sherpa-onnx, Apache-2.0), whose wheel bundles ONNX Runtime
(https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime, MIT). Its models are downloaded only when the person
presses Download in Settings › Voice, from sherpa-onnx's GitHub releases: Moonshine Base, English
(https://github.com/moonshine-ai/moonshine, Useful Sensors, MIT), and Nemotron Speech Streaming
0.6B, English (https://huggingface.co/nvidia/nemotron-speech-streaming-en-0.6b, NVIDIA, NVIDIA
Open Model License).

The "Hey Row-Bot" wake word model (src/row_bot/voice/hey_row_bot.onnx) is licensed under the
Apache License, Version 2.0. Row-Bot fine-tuned it, with LiveKit WakeWord's training code
(https://github.com/livekit/livekit-wakeword, Apache-2.0), from LiveKit, Inc.'s pretrained encoder
livekit-wakeword-encoder-standard (Copyright 2025-2026 LiveKit, Inc., Apache License 2.0). Its
training data: synthetic speech from Kokoro v1.0 (https://github.com/thewh1teagle/kokoro-onnx,
Apache-2.0) and from Piper's en_US-libritts_r-medium generator
(https://github.com/rhasspy/piper-sample-generator, MIT), trained on LibriTTS-R (Koizumi et al.,
https://www.openslr.org/141/, CC BY 4.0); background speech from LibriSpeech (Panayotov et al.,
https://www.openslr.org/12/, CC BY 4.0); noise from MS-SNSD (https://github.com/microsoft/MS-SNSD,
Microsoft), only its Freesound clips (CC0); and generated noise and rooms.
